1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sheikh Yusuf Al-Makasari''': 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1)	After growing up, Yusuf married one of Alauddin's daughters, Sitti Daeng Nisanga. According to Hamid, this marriage was a fake marriage initiated by the sultan to trick the empress (p. 132).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2)	'''Syekh Yusuf Al-Makasari''' was born in '''Gowa, South Sulawesi''', under the name Muhammad Yusuf on '''July 3, 1626'''. '''This name is said to have been given by Sultan Alauddin (1593-1639)''', the '''first ruler of Gowa to
2 CONC  embrace Islam'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3)	Regardless of the status of biological or adopted children, '''Muhammad Yusuf often introduced himself as Alauddin's son'''. One of them is quoted from the book Syekh Yusuf Guiding Us to Heaven (2008: 26) by Siradjuddin Bantang
2 CONC :
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[...] I am Yusuf, from the Kingdom of Gowa, son of Karaeng Sombaya Ri Butta Gowa I Mangarangi Daeng Manrabia with the title Sultan Alauddin, King Gowa 14th.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 4)	The second version believes that Muhammad Yusuf was Alauddin's adopted son. Samsul Munir Amin in the book Karomah Para Kiai (2008) mentions that '''Yusuf's mother is I Tubiana Daeng Kunjung''', who is still a descendant of the 
2 CONC kings of Gowa, but not Alauddin's wife (p. 227).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5)	According to Azyumardi Azra in Muhammad Yusuf Al-Maqassari (2004), Yusuf was the third pioneer of Islamic reform in the archipelago in the 17th century after Nuruddin ar-Raniri and Abdurrauf as-Singkili (p. 259). As is well kno
2 CONC wn, Yusuf was a direct student of Nuruddin ar-Raniri
2 CONT
2 CONT 6)	The name Sheikh Yusuf Al-Makasari made an impression on the people of South Africa. Nelson Mandela even referred to Yusuf as "Son of Africa, our model warrior ". In addition, as revealed by Suryana Sudrajat in Ulama Fighters an
2 CONC d Ulama Adventure: Learning Wisdom from a Country Over the Wind (2006), Mandela also made this ulama from the archipelago an inspiration for the struggle of the people of South Africa against apartheid (p. Xiv).
2 CONT
2 CONT 7)	Syekh Yusuf Al-Makasari died in Cape Town on May 23, 1699, today 319 years ago. At the request of the Sultan of Gowa, Abdul Jalil (1677-1709), Syekh Yusuf's body was repatriated to Makassar in 1705. Then his body was buried in 
2 CONC the aristocratic grave complex in Lakiung, which was once the seat of government of the Sultanate of Gowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8)	On August 7, 1995, President Soeharto on behalf of the Indonesian government appointed Syekh Yusuf Al-Makasari as a National Hero.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Shaykh Yusuf''' ['Abidin Tadia Tjoessoep] was born in 1626 of noble birth in Goa in the East Indies. He fought alongside and supported Sultan Ajung of Bantam, Goa, in his war against the Dutch. Twice Shaykh Yusuf escaped from D
2 CONC utch custody in the East, but was finally persuaded in 1694 to surrender on the promise of a pardon. The Dutch did not fulfil their promise and Shaykh Yusuf was banished, along with his family and followers, to the Castle in Batav
2 CONC ia from where he was transferred, under armed guard, to the Castle in Colombo, Ceylon [now Sri Lanka]. Fearing Shaykh Yusuf's influence in Ceylon the Dutch exiled him to the Cape of Good Hope ten years after his initial surrender.
2 CONC  The Shaykh arrived on board 'De Voetboog' on April 02, 1694 along with his retinue of 49 which '''included his two wives''' ['''Carecontoe and Carepane'''], two slave girls '''[Mu'minah and Na'imah'''], 12 children, 12 imams [rel
2 CONC igious leaders] and several friends with their families. He was royally welcomed by Governor Simon van der Stel at the Cape. They were housed on a farm in Zandvleit, near the mouth of the Eerste River in the Cape, far from Cape To
2 CONC wn, on June 14, 1694. The Company's attempt to isolate Shaykh Yusuf at Zandvleit did not succeed. On the contrary, Zandvleit turned out to be the rallying point for 'fugitive' slaves and other exiles from (he East. It was here tha
2 CONC t the first cohesive Muslim community in South Africa was established. Since many of the Shaykh's followers hailed from Macassar, the district around Zandvleit is still known today as Macassar.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.sahistory.org.za/archive/1652-1699
2 CONT
2 CONT Other Easterners taken as slaves were Muslim political leaders who objected to Dutch domination in the East Indies, perhaps the most well-known being Shaykh Yusuf, whose kramat near Faure is today an important pilgrimage destinati
2 CONC on for South African Muslims. It is still not known whether Yusuf's remains lie in the tomb or were transported back to Macassar, as the Dutch government reported, but some of his descendants did remain. One of his grandsons marri
2 CONC ed Marie Jordaan, whose origins were in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Read more: http://www.southafrica.info/about/history/mixedmarriages-genealogy.htm#.VJMWnsgA#ixzz3MLjM9IiZ
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The controversy that the bodily remains of Sh. Yusuf of Macassar was returned to Batavia or not, was finally going to be laid to rest by the late Achmat Davids in his last intended publication, "Slaves, Saints, Convicts and Pri
2 CONC nces". In a personal communication with Kammie, just a few months prior his death, Achmat Davids championed the viewpoint that the bodily remains of Sh Yusuf is still interned at the Faure Kramat. According to Achmat Davids, if I 
2 CONC correctly recollect was the fact that the local Cape Muslim community has been visiting his grave for several centuries as part of the local custom of paying homage to Sh Yusuf',''' before going on pilgrimage to Mecca. [personal c
2 CONC ommunication] Other tentative evidence is the fact that Sh Yusuf died on 23 May 1699, whilst his surviving family left the Cape in 1704 i.e. nearly 3-4 years after his demise. This must be measured against unverified contemporary 
2 CONC reports whereby 6000 Indonesians visit the national shrine of Sh Yusuf of Macassar on a daily basis ! No info on the number of visitors to the other tomb/kramat in his memory in Ceylon/Sri Lanka.
2 CONT
2 CONT Others are of the opinion that in response to a petition of the Rajah of Goa to Dutch Governor-General and Council in Batavia, the [bodily] remains of Sh. Yusuf was allowed to be removed to Batavia when the Company permitted the s
2 CONC olely Shaykh's widows, and daughters to be transferred to the East in 1704, whilst Sh Yusuf himself was buried on 23 May 1699 at Faure. His surviving entourage returned to Batavia on the two ships, De Liefde and De Speigel. In the
2 CONC  case of his sons and grandsons, only those below the age of five/six were permitted to accompany 'returning entourage' of the late Sh Yusuf of Macassar. At the same time, the Company resolved to retain his friends and servants un
2 CONC til they completed their terms of service as a measure to recoup the costs of maintaining the Sh. Yusuf and his retinue at the Cape.Two of the Shaykh's followers requested the Dutch authorities to permanently stay at the Cape, whi
2 CONC lst one of Sh. Yusuf's daughters, '''Zytia''' '''Sara Marouff, who had married the Exiled King of Tamborah at the Cape,''' '''remained behind'''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/SOUTH-AFRICA/2001-11/1004960874 http://www.awqafsa.org.za/sh-yusuf-documentary-in-the-making/
2 CONT
2 CONT https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/34660/376974.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y
2 CONT
2 CONT Abadin Tadia Tjoessoep (1626–1699) aka Sheikh Yusuf / Shaikh Yusup / Sheik(h) Joseph — den Macassarisse Priester, Schjegh Joseph — Muhammad Yūsuf al-Maqassārī (1037-1111 / 1627-99) alias Abd Allāh Abū al-Mahāsin al-Tāj al-Khalwātī
2 CONC  al-Maqassārī (known in Sulawesi as Tuanta Salamaka ri Gowa)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://mansellupham.wordpress.com/page/2/
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Shahmahomed was a wealthy educationalist and philanthropist, well-travelled and a writer. He was instrumental in the renovations of''' Shaykh Yusuf's tomb at Faure in 1927'''; the Park Road Masjid in Wynberg; and also AlÂ­Jamia Ma
2 CONC sjid in Claremont. He campaigned for a chair in Islamic Studies and Arabic at the University of Cape Town and placed in trust account a large sum of money for this purpose. He died in 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.sahistory.org.za/archive/history-muslims-south-africa-1804-1899-ebrahim-mahomed-mahida
2 CONT
